{
    "version": "1.9.14",
    "changelog": "Three things, all from Joshua's hands-on in 1.9.13. (1) COMPASS: the main view now carries a compass in its bottom-right corner, and it turns with the view \u2014 turn the map (Ctrl+drag, Q/E, or the heading buttons) and the needle follows, so north is always marked. (2) SCALE RULER + MEASURE: a scale bar sits in the bottom-left of the main view, drawn at true scale \u2014 miles at wide zoom, feet as you zoom in. Click the ruler to measure: an amber banner shows you are measuring, click two points on the map (or drag a section) and the ruler reports the section's length; click the ruler again or press Escape to stop. Measuring never disturbs the roads you had selected. (3) THE VIEW IS REMEMBERED: resize the window or drag the panes (inspector column, Pieces panel, the inspector's own split), and the editor now saves the layout as you leave it \u2014 on close and quietly as you resize \u2014 and opens next time at the size and shape you left, pulled back on-screen if the display changed. Everything else is 1.9.13 unchanged: the building jump and the manifest-carrying save.",
